1
00:00:00,416 --> 00:00:02,917
Now there is more you can control here. Most

2
00:00:02,917 --> 00:00:05,065
importantly, structured output is

3
00:00:05,065 --> 00:00:06,796
interesting, but before we get there,

4
00:00:07,646 --> 00:00:10,066
I wanna dive into some other important

5
00:00:10,066 --> 00:00:10,499
settings

6
00:00:11,092 --> 00:00:13,256
because of course we also got our main app

7
00:00:13,256 --> 00:00:15,020
settings here in the bottom right corner.

8
00:00:15,421 --> 00:00:16,975
Now here you can control

9
00:00:17,665 --> 00:00:19,925
the appearance of this app, but

10
00:00:20,470 --> 00:00:23,307
you can also tweak the runtime and hardware

11
00:00:23,612 --> 00:00:24,173
settings.

12
00:00:25,103 --> 00:00:26,369
Runtime simply means

13
00:00:26,946 --> 00:00:30,120
which underlying tool is being used for

14
00:00:30,313 --> 00:00:31,659
loading and running these

15
00:00:32,317 --> 00:00:34,017
And as I mentioned way earlier in the

16
00:00:34,017 --> 00:00:36,969
course, LM Studio, like Ollama, typically

17
00:00:36,969 --> 00:00:38,349
uses Llama CPP.

18
00:00:39,151 --> 00:00:41,959
Now, if you are on a macOS device, on a

19
00:00:41,959 --> 00:00:42,472
modern

20
00:00:43,066 --> 00:00:46,355
Apple Silicon Mac device, you could also use

21
00:00:46,435 --> 00:00:49,419
MLX as an alternative engine. And you see

22
00:00:49,419 --> 00:00:52,035
that I got both engines installed here.

23
00:00:52,869 --> 00:00:54,906
And which engine will be used depends on

24
00:00:54,955 --> 00:00:57,233
which file is loaded, which model file.

25
00:00:57,986 --> 00:01:02,041
In my case here, this is a GGUF file

26
00:01:03,211 --> 00:01:04,701
and therefore it will actually

27
00:01:05,598 --> 00:01:06,175
use the

28
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:07,986
Llama CPP runtime

29
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:11,271
and I can't change this here. But if I had

30
00:01:11,383 --> 00:01:14,605
downloaded an MLX file and some models exist

31
00:01:14,669 --> 00:01:18,098
in this format, then this MLX runtime would

32
00:01:18,098 --> 00:01:20,197
be used. So that's just nice to know.

33
00:01:21,303 --> 00:01:23,515
You can theoretically also manage the

34
00:01:23,515 --> 00:01:25,710
versions of that runtime if you knew that

35
00:01:25,710 --> 00:01:28,066
you wanted to switch to a very specific

36
00:01:28,114 --> 00:01:28,707
version, but

37
00:01:29,236 --> 00:01:31,464
in reality, it's unlikely that you would

38
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:32,698
want to change something there.

39
00:01:33,276 --> 00:01:35,408
Still interesting to see that multiple

40
00:01:35,408 --> 00:01:37,652
runtimes may exist depending on your

41
00:01:37,732 --> 00:01:39,752
operating system and underlying hardware.

42
00:01:40,474 --> 00:01:42,846
Speaking of hardware, that's also something

43
00:01:42,878 --> 00:01:44,770
that can be inspected here in the settings.

44
00:01:45,187 --> 00:01:47,704
Specifically, if you had multiple GPUs, you

45
00:01:47,752 --> 00:01:48,602
could control

46
00:01:49,131 --> 00:01:50,926
which GPUs are used for

47
00:01:51,455 --> 00:01:53,572
the model, for running the model. In my

48
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:56,634
case, I only got my one Apple chip, which

49
00:01:56,890 --> 00:01:58,958
acts as the GPU. So that's the one that is

50
00:01:58,990 --> 00:01:59,487
being used.

51
00:02:00,033 --> 00:02:02,086
And if I had disabled that, then

52
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:04,574
my models would be loaded onto the CPU

53
00:02:04,622 --> 00:02:06,900
instead, which is less efficient than using

54
00:02:06,932 --> 00:02:09,628
the GPU. So you typically wanna enable your

55
00:02:09,724 --> 00:02:12,099
GPU and make sure that the models get

56
00:02:12,147 --> 00:02:12,901
executed there.

57
00:02:13,993 --> 00:02:16,239
Here you also see the available VRAM

58
00:02:16,239 --> 00:02:19,159
capacity. As mentioned earlier, those Apple

59
00:02:19,208 --> 00:02:21,294
M1 chips have something called unified

60
00:02:21,294 --> 00:02:24,391
memory, which means almost the entire RAM is

61
00:02:24,663 --> 00:02:27,038
VRAM. You also see that down here. And of

62
00:02:27,070 --> 00:02:29,748
course, you will see the values for your

63
00:02:29,973 --> 00:02:30,630
system there.

64
00:02:31,399 --> 00:02:33,724
Now, this guardrails part is also

65
00:02:33,804 --> 00:02:36,498
interesting. The idea here is that you can

66
00:02:36,530 --> 00:02:37,043
control

67
00:02:37,877 --> 00:02:40,827
whether LM Studio allows you to load models

68
00:02:41,244 --> 00:02:42,751
into your memory

69
00:02:43,408 --> 00:02:45,909
that shouldn't be loaded into memory or not.

70
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:49,324
The default is strict, and that is generally

71
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:51,168
what you should keep enabled.

72
00:02:52,162 --> 00:02:54,968
But if you knew that you wanted to load a

73
00:02:54,968 --> 00:02:58,432
pretty large model, into memory and you knew

74
00:02:58,641 --> 00:03:00,631
that it would work and that you would only

75
00:03:00,663 --> 00:03:02,316
use it in a way that's no problem,

76
00:03:03,054 --> 00:03:04,868
you could set this to off or

77
00:03:05,397 --> 00:03:08,494
more relaxed so that you're able to squeeze

78
00:03:08,848 --> 00:03:11,784
bigger models into your limited resources.

79
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:14,801
That is something you should do with care

80
00:03:15,090 --> 00:03:17,273
though, because of course you can overload

81
00:03:17,273 --> 00:03:19,632
your system here and potentially crash your

82
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:22,809
system. So I do recommend sticking to strict

83
00:03:22,809 --> 00:03:22,922
here,

84
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:26,081
But if you had the knowledge and you had the

85
00:03:26,081 --> 00:03:28,437
advanced use case that you needed to

86
00:03:29,047 --> 00:03:30,826
basically go beyond the limits,

87
00:03:31,371 --> 00:03:35,123
you could disable or relax those guardrails.

88
00:03:36,197 --> 00:03:38,778
You can also choose custom to control how

89
00:03:38,778 --> 00:03:41,552
much memory may be used for the model to be

90
00:03:41,568 --> 00:03:44,261
loaded. So that also allows you to go into

91
00:03:44,293 --> 00:03:46,986
the other direction. You might know that of

92
00:03:47,018 --> 00:03:48,269
the available memory,

93
00:03:48,878 --> 00:03:51,075
48 gigabytes of VRAM in my case,

94
00:03:51,668 --> 00:03:53,063
you only wanna use, let's say,

95
00:03:53,817 --> 00:03:56,066
10 gigabytes at most for a model,

96
00:03:56,629 --> 00:03:59,039
you could then set a custom guardrail to

97
00:03:59,039 --> 00:03:59,778
limit that.

98
00:04:00,501 --> 00:04:02,799
And any model that would require more memory

99
00:04:02,896 --> 00:04:04,808
would be blocked from being loaded.

100
00:04:05,563 --> 00:04:07,572
And that can of course be useful if you also

101
00:04:07,572 --> 00:04:10,031
have other processes running on your system

102
00:04:10,464 --> 00:04:12,762
that need a significant amount of VRAM.

103
00:04:13,919 --> 00:04:15,398
Now here I'll go back to strict.
